::GEO_U05_L06_Q01::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A sector in diagram 1 has radius 6 and central angle 60Â°. Which expression gives its arc length?
{
=A. 60/360(2pi6)#Arc length is the fraction of the circumference determined by the central angle: Î¸/360(2pir).
~B. 60/360(pi6^2)#This is the sector area structure, not arc length.
~C. 2pi6#This is the full circumference, not the arc for a 60Â° sector.
~D. pi6^2#This is the full circle area, not an arc length.
}


::GEO_U05_L06_Q02::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A sector in diagram 2 has radius 8 and central angle 90Â°. Which expression gives its sector area?
{
~A. 90/360(2pi8)#This is the arc length structure, not sector area.
=B. 90/360(pi8^2)#Sector area is the central-angle fraction of the circle area.
~C. 2pi8#This is circumference, not area.
~D. 90pi#This ignores the radius and the fraction of the circle.
}


::GEO_U05_L06_Q03::
Question ID: GEO_U05_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CIR.7
Question:
A 120Â° arc has radius 9. What coefficient of pi gives the arc length?
{
~A. 9pi#This uses the radius but not the central-angle fraction.
~B. 120pi#This uses the angle as a length coefficient.
=C. 6pi#Use 120/360(2pi9), so the coefficient of pi is 6.
~D. Cannot be determined#The radius and central angle are given, so the arc length coefficient can be determined.
}


